-# Retail Analytics · SQL Query Book
+# Retail SQL analytics
 
-Ten analytical SQL queries for an e-commerce business — CTEs, window
-functions, cohort analysis, and RFM segmentation — run against a
-purpose-built SQLite database of ~45,000 rows across five tables.
+Ten executable SQLite analyses over generated retail data: 5,000 customers, 500 products, 10 categories, 11,430 orders and 28,478 order lines. The observed orders cover September 2024 through August 2026. All records and business outcomes are synthetic.
 
-## Schema
+## Run
 
-```
-customers (5,000)      products (500)          categories (10)
-   customer_id ─┐         product_id ─┐            category_id
-   name         │         name        │            name
-   country      │         category_id ─────────────┘
-   signup_date  │         unit_price
-                │         cost
-                │
-   orders (11,430)          order_items (28,478)
-      order_id ─┼─────────────── order_id
-      customer_id                 product_id ────► products
-      order_date                  quantity
-                                  unit_price
+Install this folder's `requirements.txt` or the repository-wide requirements:
+
+```sh
+python build_db.py
+python run_queries.py
 ```
 
-Data span: 24 months (2024-09 through 2026-09). Signup dates and
-purchase timing are staggered so cohort and retention queries produce
-non-trivial curves.
+`build_db.py` replaces the local generated `retail.db`; the database is not committed. Foreign-key enforcement is enabled during generation. `run_queries.py` writes ten CSVs to `results/`.
 
-## Queries
+## Query guide
 
-| # | Business question | SQL technique |
+| Query | Question | Definition or caveat |
 |---|---|---|
-| Q1  | Monthly revenue and MoM growth | `LAG()` window |
-| Q2  | Top-10 products with rank and share | `RANK()` + `SUM() OVER ()` |
-| Q3  | Category revenue with running total | `SUM() OVER (ROWS ...)` |
-| Q4  | Customer LTV decile breakdown | `NTILE(10)` |
-| Q5  | RFM customer segmentation | 3 × `NTILE(5)` + CASE |
-| Q6  | Cohort retention by signup month | Self-join + month math |
-| Q7  | Repeat-purchase rate by country | Conditional aggregation |
-| Q8  | Top-2 SKU per category | `ROW_NUMBER() PARTITION BY` |
-| Q9  | Average basket value & margin/month | Multi-table join, margin math |
-| Q10 | Days between purchases per customer | `LAG()` inside partitions |
-
-## Sample outputs
-
-**Q5 — RFM segmentation** (from `results/q5.csv`)
-
-| segment | customers | avg_monetary | avg_frequency | avg_recency_days |
-|---|---|---|---|---|
-| Champions       | 798  | 546.91 | 4.09 | 18.3 |
-| At Risk         | 476  | 467.21 | 3.64 | 190.3 |
-| Can't Lose Them | 114  | 402.66 | 1.89 | 247.5 |
-| Needs Attention | 1,184 | 304.23 | 2.57 | 110.9 |
-| Loyal           | 480  | 286.85 | 2.54 | 21.0 |
-| New / Recent    | 532  | 132.56 | 1.42 | 22.1 |
-| Hibernating     | 940  | 126.76 | 1.28 | 275.6 |
-
-**Q1 — Monthly revenue** (first 6 months from `results/q1.csv`)
-
-| month | revenue | orders | active_customers | MoM growth |
-|---|---|---|---|---|
-| 2024-09 | 1,275   | 12  | 12  | – |
-| 2024-10 | 4,868   | 33  | 31  | +281.7 % |
-| 2024-11 | 5,965   | 50  | 48  |  +22.5 % |
-| 2024-12 | 6,880   | 64  | 60  |  +15.4 % |
-| 2025-01 | 14,772  | 113 | 103 | +114.7 % |
-| 2025-02 | 14,574  | 112 | 107 |   −1.3 % |
+| Q1 | Monthly revenue and growth | Distinct orders/customers; first growth value is NULL |
+| Q2 | Top products | Share denominator includes all product revenue |
+| Q3 | Category revenue | Running sum and share of total |
+| Q4 | Customer spend deciles | Observed spend among purchasers, not predicted lifetime value |
+| Q5 | RFM segments | Snapshot at 2026-09-01; purchaser-only population; deterministic customer-ID tie breaks |
+| Q6 | Signup-cohort activity | All signups in denominator; not-yet-observed horizons remain NULL |
+| Q7 | Repeat purchases by country | At least two orders / all signed-up customers |
+| Q8 | Two leading products per category | Per-category window ranking |
+| Q9 | Basket value and gross margin | Generated catalog prices/costs; not net profit |
+| Q10 | Days between orders | Counts all orders, including the first purchase; average uses only consecutive gaps |
 
-## Repo layout
-
-```
-sql_retail/
-├── build_db.py             # generates retail.db (~45k rows)
-├── queries.sql             # the 10 analytical queries
-├── run_queries.py          # runs each query and writes results/*.csv
-├── retail.db               # SQLite database (created by build_db.py)
-└── results/                # one CSV per query
-    ├── q1.csv … q10.csv
-```
-
-## Reproducing
-
-```bash
-python3 build_db.py     # creates retail.db
-python3 run_queries.py  # writes results/q1..q10.csv
-```
+Q6 rates are activity among the signup cohort at a given month offset, not retention of month-zero purchasers. Horizon eligibility is based on the last observed calendar month; a real production report should use an explicit data-completeness watermark. Do not treat an unobserved future month as zero activity. Q10 orders on the same date are ordered by order_id to keep the window deterministic.
 
-Queries are ANSI SQL and run unchanged on PostgreSQL apart from the
-SQLite-specific `strftime` / `julianday` date functions (swap for
-`TO_CHAR` / date subtraction in Postgres).
+These queries use SQLite-specific `strftime`, `julianday`, and date modifiers. Port the date expressions and validate outputs before using PostgreSQL; unchanged cross-database compatibility is not claimed.
 
-## Tech
+## Checks
 
-SQLite 3 · Python 3.12 · pandas (results only)
+The repository regression suite verifies database integrity, foreign keys, revenue reconciliation across queries, full customer counts in Q10, cohort denominators, and NULL future horizons. See the top-level README for commands.
